# Security Model Overview

Security in Track Binocle is not a single feature — it is a set of walls, each
independent, each verified by its own gate. If one wall is bypassed the next one
still holds. This page is the map; every wall below has its own page.

> [!info] The core principle
> Nothing is public by default. Every request is authenticated, every read and
> write is scoped to the caller, and the owner is derived from the credential —
> never from a path or request body.

## The walls, outside in

| Wall | What it stops | Details |
|:-----|:--------------|:--------|
| **TLS + local CA** | Traffic snooping, MITM | [TLS Certs & Local CA](tls-certs-local-ca.xmd) |
| **Identity** | Anonymous access | short-lived JWT + refresh-token rotation |
| **Authorization** | Doing more than you may | route guards, RBAC, per-request scoping |
| **Row-Level Security** | Cross-tenant data reads | [Row-Level Security](row-level-security.xmd) |
| **Secrets isolation** | Leaked credentials | [Vault & Secrets](vault-secrets-architecture.xmd) |
| **Supply chain** | Malicious dependencies | [Supply-Chain Lockdown](supply-chain-lockdown.xmd) |

## Identity & authorization

- **JWT access tokens** are short-lived; **refresh tokens rotate on each use** —
  a stolen refresh token is invalidated the moment the real one is used.
- Sessions are invalidated on password change and on any detected anomaly.
- Passwords are hashed with **bcrypt** (12+ salt rounds).
- Every protected endpoint carries a **route-level guard** — no endpoint is
  public by default. Roles are checked at the guard layer (RBAC), never in a
  controller or a DTO.

## The data wall

The Kong anonymous API key is [color=#b5532e]public by design[/color], and there
is no Kong ACL plugin on the REST surface. That makes **Postgres RLS the only
data wall** — a deliberate architecture that the
[RLS hardening migration](gdpr-auth-security-migrations.xmd) closes findings
against. This is the single most important sentence on this page.

> [!warning] Never trust client-supplied ownership
> The owner of a row is derived from the credential, not the request. There is no
> cross-owner access *by construction* — an attacker cannot ask for another
> tenant's `{id}` and receive it.

## Verified, not claimed

Security here follows one rule: **UNKNOWN = FAIL**. A wall is only "up" when a
gate proves it. The gates that back the walls above:

- [ ] Manual pen-test or a compliance framework — *explicitly out of scope*
- [x] `make baas-security-scan` — SAST + SCA + container + secret scan
- [x] `mini-baas-security` code-scanning driven to **zero** open alerts
- [x] `supply-chain` — frozen lockfiles, no lifecycle scripts
- [x] `fresh-machine` — clean clone → `make all` comes up green

> [!note] Scope
> This is a 42 school project. Formal penetration testing, SOC 2 / ISO 27001, a
> bug-bounty program, and 24/7 incident response are all out of scope. The
> practices here reflect what a production service at this scale actually needs —
> security as a technical discipline, not compliance theater.

## Reporting a vulnerability

Do **not** open a public GitHub issue. Report privately to the Tech Lead or
Project Manager via Discord DM, with what the vulnerability is, where it lives,
how to reproduce it, and the impact.

| Step | Deadline |
|:-----|:---------|
| Acknowledgment | 24 hours |
| Initial assessment | 48 hours |
| Fix — critical severity | 1 week |
| Fix — medium severity | 2 weeks |
